        "findings": [
          {
            "source_quote": "ὡς θεοφιλεῖς",
            "english_quote": "as friends of God",
            "issue": "θεοφιλεῖς denotes people dear to or beloved by God. “Friends of God” is less source-transparent and blurs the distinction between the preceding friendship directed toward God, εἰς θεὸν φιλίαν, and this description of divine favor. The notes do not document that lexical flattening.",
            "suggested_revision": "Replace “as friends of God” with “as people dear to God” or “as people beloved by God.” Submit the revised wording for fresh review.",
            "severity": "minor"
          },
          {
            "source_quote": "τοὺς κεραυνῷ θνήσκοντας",
            "english_quote": "those who die by lightning",
            "issue": "The recurring κεραυνός is rendered “thunderbolt” in the account of the soul’s summons but “lightning” here and in the subsequent negated occurrence, μὴ κεραυνῷ θνησκόντων. These occurrences describe the same manner of death, and no contextual reason for varying this central term is documented. The variation also weakens the observable distinction between ἀστραπή and κεραυνός.",
            "suggested_revision": "Use “those who die by a thunderbolt” here and “even when those people had not died by a thunderbolt” later. Compare “By this lightning, then, which fell from heaven to earth” for the opening ἀστραπῆς, retaining the source’s two terms. These proposed changes require fresh review.",
            "severity": "minor"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
